  <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="section-heading">Introduction</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Three federal taxes are paid on wages and self-employment income—income tax, social security tax, and Medicare tax. Social security and Medicare taxes are collected under one of two systems. Under the Self-Employment Contributions Act (SECA), the self-employed pay both the employee and employer portions of the taxes. Under Internal Revenue Code (IRC), the employee and the employer each pay half of the taxes. No matter which system is used, the taxes are paid to both systems.</p>
          <p>This publication contains information for the following classes of taxpayers:</p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li class="list-item">Members of a religious order.</li>
            <li class="list-item">Christian Science practitioners and leaders.</li>
            <li class="list-item">Religious workers (church employees).</li>
            <li class="list-item">Members of a recognized religious sect.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Note. Unless otherwise noted in this publication, references to members of the clergy in international contexts, members of religious orders, and Christian Science practitioners are not covered by the SE tax.</p>
          <p>This publication covers the following topics about the collection of social security and Medicare taxes from members of the clergy, religious workers, and members of a recognized religious sect.</p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li class="list-item">Which earnings are taxed under FICA and which under SECA. See Table 1.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="section-heading">Ministerial Services</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Ministerial services. In general, the service in the exercise of your duties as required by your religious order or sect is not subject to social security and Medicare taxes. See Table 1.</p>
          <p>The following provisions describe more detail on ministerial services of ministers of religious orders and sects:</p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li class="list-item">The service of a minister of a religious order or sect is not subject to social security and Medicare taxes.</li>
            <li class="list-item">The service of a minister of a religious order or sect is not subject to social security and Medicare taxes.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Ministers. Ministers are individuals who are ordained, commissioned, or licensed to perform religious services. The service of a minister is generally not subject to social security and Medicare taxes. See Table 1.</p>
          <p>Members of Religious Orders. Members of religious orders are individuals who are members of a religious order. The service of a member of a religious order is generally not subject to social security and Medicare taxes. See Table 1.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="section-heading">Forms and Instructions</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Tax questions. If you have a tax question, check the information available on IRS.gov or call the IRS at 1-800-829-1040. We cannot answer tax questions sent to other than the above address.</p>
          <p>Useful items. You may want to see:</p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li class="list-item">Publication <span data-field="pub_number_1">{{ pub_number_1 }}</span> Tax Guide for U.S. Citizens and Resident Aliens Abroad</li>
            <li class="list-item">Publication <span data-field="pub_number_2">{{ pub_number_2 }}</span> Taxable and Nontaxable Income</li>
            <li class="list-item">Publication <span data-field="pub_number_3">{{ pub_number_3 }}</span> Business Expenses</li>
            <li class="list-item">Publication <span data-field="pub_number_4">{{ pub_number_4 }}</span> Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)</li>
            <li class="list-item">Publication <span data-field="pub_number_5">{{ pub_number_5 }}</span> Earned Income Credit (EIC)</li>
            <li class="list-item">Form <span data-field="form_number_1">{{ form_number_1 }}</span> Determination of Worker Status for Purposes of Federal Employment Taxes and Income Tax Withholding</li>
            <li class="list-item">Schedule <span data-field="schedule_number_1">{{ schedule_number_1 }}</span> Certificate of Election of Coverage Under Federal Insurance Contributions Act</li>
            <li class="list-item">Schedule <span data-field="schedule_number_2">{{ schedule_number_2 }}</span> (Form 1040) Profit or Loss From Business (Sole Proprietorship)</li>
            <li class="list-item">Schedule <span data-field="schedule_number_3">{{ schedule_number_3 }}</span> (Form 1040) Net Profit From Business (Sole Proprietorship)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="section-heading">Exemption From Self-Employment (SE) Tax</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Exemption from SE tax. You can request an exemption from SE tax if you meet all of the following conditions:</p>
          <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>You are a member of a religious order or sect.</li>
            <li>You are a minister of a religious order or sect.</li>
            <li>You are a member of a recognized religious sect.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>You must file Form <span data-field="form_number_2">{{ form_number_2 }}</span> to request an exemption.</p>
          <p>What cannot be exempt. You cannot be exempt from SE tax if you made one of the following types of earnings:</p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li class="list-item">Earnings from self-employment that are not ministerial services.</li>
            <li class="list-item">Earnings from a business that is not a religious order or sect.</li>
            <li class="list-item">Earnings from a religious order or sect that are not ministerial services.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>If you are a minister, member of a religious order, or member of a recognized religious sect, you may be exempt from SE tax. See Form <span data-field="form_number_3">{{ form_number_3 }}</span>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="section-heading">Requesting Exemption—Form 4029</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>To request exemption from SE tax, file Form <span data-field="form_number_4">{{ form_number_4 }}</span> with the IRS.</p>
          <p>The IRS will return to you a copy of the form you filed, along with a notice of your exemption, if it is approved. If it is approved, keep the approved copy in your permanent records.</p>
          <p>When to file. File Form <span data-field="form_number_5">{{ form_number_5 }}</span> by the due date of your tax return, or as soon as possible after the due date if you are filing an extension.</p>
          <p>Example 1. Rev. Lawrence Jaeger, a clergyman, filed Form <span data-field="form_number_6">{{ form_number_6 }}</span> in <span data-field="year_ex1">{{ year_ex1 }}</span>. He met all of the conditions for exemption. The IRS approved his exemption. He kept the approved copy in his records.</p>
          <p>Example 2. Rev. Louise Wolfe has $300 in self-employment earnings. She filed Form <span data-field="form_number_7">{{ form_number_7 }}</span> in <span data-field="year_ex2">{{ year_ex2 }}</span>. The IRS approved her exemption. She kept the approved copy in her records.</p>
          <p>Example 3. Rev. David Ross was ordained and ministered for a religious order. He filed Form <span data-field="form_number_8">{{ form_number_8 }}</span> in <span data-field="year_ex3">{{ year_ex3 }}</span>. The IRS approved his exemption. He kept the approved copy in his records.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="section-heading">Self-Employment Tax: Figuring Net Earnings</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>There are two methods for figuring your net earnings from self-employment: Regular Method and Nonaffiliates Method.</p>
          <div class="sub-heading">Regular Method</div>
          <p>Most people use the regular method. Under this method, you calculate your net earnings from self-employment by taking your gross income for self-employment and subtracting your expenses. You then multiply your net earnings by <span data-field="multiplier">{{ multiplier }}</span>. Use Schedule SE (Form 1040) to figure your net earnings.</p>
          <div class="sub-heading">Nonaffiliates Method</div>
          <p>If you are an employee of a church that elects to exclude you from FICA coverage, you may use the nonaffiliates method. This method allows you to calculate your net earnings from self-employment by taking your gross income for self-employment and subtracting your expenses. You then multiply your net earnings by <span data-field="multiplier_non">{{ multiplier_non }}</span>. Do not use this method if you are an employee of a church that does not elect to exclude you from FICA coverage.</p>
          <p>Amounts included in gross income. To figure your net earnings from self-employment, include:</p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li class="list-item">All income from your self-employment.</li>
            <li class="list-item">Any income from a religious order or sect.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="page">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="section-heading">Members of the Clergy</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>To claim the exemption from SE tax, you must meet all of the following conditions:</p>
          <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>You file Form <span data-field="form_number_9">{{ form_number_9 }}</span>, described later under Pub. 517, for the year you are claiming the exemption.</li>
            <li>You are conscientiously opposed to public service or are a minister of a religious order or sect.</li>
            <li>You are a member of a recognized religious sect.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>You must also meet the requirements for the exemption under <span data-field="reference_link">{{ reference_link }}</span>.</p>
          <p>For example, if you are directed to perform services for a religious order or sect, you may be exempt from SE tax. However, if you are directed to work outside the religious order or sect, the exemption will not be considered. Services are considered ministerial if they are performed by members of the order, or if they are performed by members of the order under the order's direction.</p>
          <p>Services are considered ministerial if they are performed by members of the order, or if they are performed by members of the order under the order's direction. Services performed by members of the order outside the order's direction are not considered ministerial.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="section-heading">Members of Religious Sects</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Members of a recognized religious sect. If you are a member of a recognized religious sect, you may claim an exemption from SE tax. You must file Form <span data-field="form_number_10">{{ form_number_10 }}</span>.</p>
          <p>Exemption. If you received social security benefits, you may not claim an exemption from SE tax. If you are a member of a recognized religious sect, you may claim an exemption from SE tax, but you must not have received social security benefits.</p>
          <p>Eligibility requirements. To claim this exemption, you must:</p>
          <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>File Form <span data-field="form_number_11">{{ form_number_11 }}</span>, described later under Pub. 517.</li>
            <li>Be a member of a recognized religious sect.</li>
            <li>Not have received social security benefits.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>The Commissioner of Social Security must issue a letter to you confirming that you have not received social security benefits.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

