<body>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Outside Earnings</h2>
  <p>If you are a member of a religious organization and you give your outside earnings to the organization, you may be able to deduct your income. However, you may be entitled to a charitable contribution deduction for the amount paid to the organization. For more information, see Publication 556.</p>

  <h2 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Exclusion of Rental Allowance and Fair Rental Value of a Parsonage</h2>
  <p>Ordained, commissioned, or licensed ministers of the gospel may be able to exclude from income the fair rental value of a parsonage provided to them as pay for their services. See Instructions.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 20px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">General Rules</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Administrative duties and teaching at theological seminaries, and</li>
        <li>The ordinary duties of a minister performed as an employee of the United States (other than as a chaplain in the Armed Forces), a state, a political subdivision, political or of the District of Columbia.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <div class="tip-box">
        <h4 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">TIP</h4>
        <p>If you live in a facility lodging as an emeritus or retired educator, the fair rental value of that lodging may be nontaxable under the rules in Publication 556. See instructions for applying utility lodging in the discussion on meals and lodging under Fringe Benefits.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top: 20px;">This exclusion applies only for income tax purposes. It does not apply for SE tax purposes. See the section under Unearned Income in the section on Self-Employment Tax: Figuring Net Earnings.</p>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Designation Requirement.</h3>
  <p>The church or organization that employs you must officially designate the parsonage as a residence. If the parsonage is not the primary residence, the amount of the housing allowance may be subject to income tax. If you are employed and paid by a local or county organization, a resolution by a national church governing body of your denomination must effectively designate a housing allowance for you. The local congregation must officially designate the part of your salary that is to be a housing allowance. If no such designation is made, the church agency can designate your housing allowance if you are directly employed by the national or local agency. If no such agency exists, you must indicate your total salary in your income.</p>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Rental Allowances.</h3>
  <p>If you receive in your salary an amount officially designated as a rental allowance (including an amount paid to cover utilities), you can exclude the allowance from your gross income if:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The amount is used to provide rent or a home, and</li>
    <li>The amount is not more than reasonable for your services.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The rental allowance cannot be more than the fair rental value of the home, including furnishings, plus the cost of utilities.</p>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Fair rental value of parsonage.</h3>
  <p>You can exclude from gross income the fair rental value of a house or parsonage, including utilities, furnished or unfurnished, provided that:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The exclusion cannot be more than the reasonable pay for your services. If you pay for the utilities, you can exclude any allowance designated for utility costs, up to your actual utility costs.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Expense Items</h2>
  <p>The tax treatment of ministerial trade or business expenses, expenses allowable to tax-free income, and health insurance costs is discussed here.</p>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Ministerial Trade or Business Expenses as an Employee</h3>
  <p>When you figure your income, tax must itemize your deductions on Schedule A (Form 1040) to claim allowable deductions for ministerial trade or business expenses incurred while working as an employee. You also may have to itemize these deductions on Form 2106, Employee Business Expenses (Form 2106).</p>
  <p>These expenses are claimed as miscellaneous itemized deductions and are subject to the 2% of adjusted-gross income (AGI) limit. See Publication 529 for more information on this topic.</p>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">How many of your employee business expenses that are allowable to tax-free income will not be deductible (discussed next).</h3>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Expenses Allowable to Tax-Free Income</h3>
  <p>If you receive a rental or parsonage allowance that is exempt from income tax (tax free), you must allocate a portion of the expenses of your home to the tax-free rental or parsonage allowance. You cannot deduct the portion of your expenses that is allocated to your tax-free rental or parsonage allowance.</p>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Exception.</h3>
  <p>This rule does not apply to your miscellaneous itemized deductions. See the section on Unearned Income in the section on Self-Employment Tax: Figuring Net Earnings.</p>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Figuring the allocation.</h3>
  <p>Figure the portion of your otherwise deductible expenses that you cannot deduct (because that portion must be allocated to tax-free income) by multiplying the expenses by the ratio:</p>
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 10px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <p>Tax-free rental or parsonage allowance</p>
      <p>All income (taxable and tax free) earned</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <p>Tax-free rental or parsonage allowance</p>
      <p>All income (taxable and tax free) earned</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>When figuring the allocation, include the income in which the rental or parsonage allowance is included, and the expenses related to the ministerial trade or business. If you are an employee and as a self-employed person.</p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <h4 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">TIP</h4>
    <p>Example. Rev. Charles Ashford received $40,000 in earnings for ministerial services. He received a rental allowance of $10,000. The ratio is $10,000 / $50,000 = 0.20. He cannot deduct 20% of his expenses.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Deduction for SE Tax</h2>
  <p>For 2012, you can deduct 57.51% of your SE tax in filing as a self-employed person. If you are an employee, you cannot deduct any SE tax. If you are an employee, you cannot deduct any SE tax.</p>

  <h2 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Income Tax Withholding and Estimated Tax</h2>
  <p>The federal income tax is a pay-as-you-go tax. You must pay the tax as you earn it. If you are an employee, your employer will withhold income tax from your wages. If you are self-employed, you must pay estimated taxes to the IRS throughout the year. You may also be able to claim credits for certain expenses. See Publication 529 for more information.</p>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Health Insurance Costs of Self-Employed Ministers</h3>
  <p>If you are self-employed, you may be able to deduct the amount you paid in 2012 for medical and dental insurance and qualified long-term care insurance for you, your spouse, and your dependents.</p>

  <p>If you qualify, you can take this deduction as an adjustment to income on Form 1040, line 26. See the instructions for Form 1040 to figure your deduction.</p>

  <p>The following special rules apply to the deduction for health insurance: The expenses taken into account for purposes of this deduction are not allowed on your tax return if you are an employee. If you are a self-employed person, you may be able to deduct the cost of health insurance for yourself, your spouse, and your dependents. See Publication 529 for more information.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Tax-Sheltered Annuities.</h2>
  <p>Retirement savings arrangements are subject to special rules. You can deduct contributions to certain retirement plans. You can deduct contributions to a traditional IRA, Roth IRA, or a SEP. You can also contribute to a 401(k) plan. See Publication 590 for more information.</p>

  <h3 style="font-family: 'DejaVu Sans', sans-serif;">Retirement plans for the self-employed.</h3>
  <p>You can contribute to a SEP, SIMPLE, or qualified retirement plan for employees. You can also contribute to a traditional IRA or Roth IRA.</p>

  <p>The common-law rules determine whether you are an employee or a self-employed person.</p>

